WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.208
You press K on the keyboard, and
a letter appears on the screen.

2
00:00:08.428 --> 00:00:13.954
Hardware, software, hardware: one working
system, made of parts with different jobs.

3
00:00:14.454 --> 00:00:18.077
A keyboard with no software
is just a box of buttons.

4
00:00:18.297 --> 00:00:21.037
A program with no hardware
has nowhere to run.

5
00:00:21.257 --> 00:00:25.436
Press K, and the keyboard, hardware,
sends a signal down the wire.

6
00:00:25.656 --> 00:00:29.557
The operating system and the app,
software, decide what it means.

7
00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:35.304
The screen, hardware, shows the K. Hardware
and software combine into one system.

8
00:00:35.524 --> 00:00:40.864
An e-reader is a system too: hardware,
plus software that shows the page.

9
00:00:41.084 --> 00:00:47.307
No software: a screen shows nothing.
No screen: software has nowhere to go.

10
00:00:47.807 --> 00:00:53.055
Hardware is the parts you can touch:
it senses, shows, stores and sounds.

11
00:00:53.275 --> 00:00:59.451
Showing a picture: the screen is hardware.
Deciding which picture: that is software.

12
00:00:59.671 --> 00:01:04.826
Software is the instructions: it decides,
calculates and works out what to do.

13
00:01:05.046 --> 00:01:11.269
Storing a file: the hard drive is hardware.
The program that saves it: software.

14
00:01:11.489 --> 00:01:17.062
A program that works out 37 plus 48
is software, running on the processor.

15
00:01:17.282 --> 00:01:21.647
Every job the computer does has a
hardware half and a software half.

16
00:01:22.147 --> 00:01:25.630
An input can be manual: a
person does it themself.

17
00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:32.305
A keyboard, a mouse, a touchscreen, a
microphone you speak into: all manual.

18
00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:37.959
An input can be automatic: a sensor
does it by itself, with no person.

19
00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:44.959
A thermometer, a light sensor, a barcode
scanner: each senses without being asked.

20
00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:49.823
The sensor on an automatic door spots
you and opens it, all by itself.

21
00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:56.870
Automatic does not mean better; it just means
no person. Each kind is right for its job.

22
00:01:57.090 --> 00:02:02.802
Automatic inputs read you: a fingerprint
or a face. That is biometric.

23
00:02:03.022 --> 00:02:08.780
Others read a tag: RFID. You wave
it, like a bus pass, to get on.

24
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:12.810
The hard drive keeps files,
and every file has a type.

25
00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:19.903
A text file holds words, like a story.
An audio file holds sound, like a song.

26
00:02:20.123 --> 00:02:26.021
An image file holds a picture. A video
file holds moving pictures with sound.

27
00:02:26.241 --> 00:02:29.027
A game file holds a
whole game, ready to run.

28
00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:35.377
Record yourself singing, and the file
that is saved is audio: it holds sound.

29
00:02:35.597 --> 00:02:40.195
The type decides what the file
holds, and which program can open it.

30
00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:44.271
Hardware and software combine
into one working system.

31
00:02:44.491 --> 00:02:49.553
Hardware senses, shows and stores.
Software decides and calculates.

32
00:02:49.773 --> 00:02:55.299
Manual inputs need a person.
Automatic inputs sense by themselves.

33
00:02:55.519 --> 00:03:01.881
Text, audio, image, video, games:
five types of file, on the hard drive.
